    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                We can fit just about any function to our data using python.
                We just have to define the type of function we want to fit.
                For example, let's get python to fit our 'data' that has the
                shape of an exponential curve of the form <m>y=ae^{bx} + c</m>.
                Below, the code defines <m>a=2</m>, <m>b=3</m>, <m>c=0.7</m>, as
                well as <m>x</m> values between 0 and 1, and <m>y</m> values
                that have some random noise to simulate our data.
                Then we need to define the function we are trying to fit our
                data to for some unknown <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, and then we run the code
                <c>curve_fit(function we are fitting to, xdata, ydata)</c>.
                Adapt this code to do the following.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Change x-data to go from 1 to 10, and change the y-data to
                    be of the form <m>y=0.5\log(0.6x)+0.3+ \textrm{noise}</m>
                    where the factor in front of the noise is 0.2 instead of
                    1.8.
                    Then adapt the function you are trying to fit, and run the
                    code to find the best fit.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute error,
                    <m>\sqrt{\sum (y - y_{pred})^2}</m>, and the relative error,
                    <m>\frac{\sqrt{\sum (y - y_{pred})^2}}{\sqrt{\sum y^2}}</m>.
                    Do you think this is a good fit?
                    <em>Note: <c>np.sum(vector)</c>
                    will add all the entries in 'vector' together.</em>
                </p>

                <program language="python">
            <code>
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from scipy.integrate import odeint

## We may need new things...
from numpy import linalg as LA       # Linear algebra library
import seaborn as sns                # Data visualization library used for plotting
import pandas as pd                  # Data analysis library
from scipy.optimize import curve_fit # fit data

## Generate the data
a = 2
b = 3
c = 0.7
np.random.seed(3)            # this sets the random generator so it starts at the same spot
xdata = np.linspace(0,1,20)
y = a*np.exp(b*xdata)+c      # y-data before noise
ydata = y + 1.8*np.random.random(size=len(xdata)) # y-data + noise

## Define the function we want to fit to
def f(x,a,b,c):
  return a*np.exp(b*x) + c

## Fit the data
popt, pcov = curve_fit(f, xdata, ydata)  # popt gives the optimal parameter values, pcov is the covariance of popt
print('the fitted a, b, c are:', popt)

## Plot
plt.plot(xdata, f(xdata, *popt), 'r-',
         label='fit: a=%5.3f, b=%5.3f, c=%5.3f' % tuple(popt)) # Plots the fit
plt.scatter(xdata,ydata, label='Real data')                    # original data
plt.plot(xdata, y,'g:', label='Curve without noise') # what the curve would have been without noise
plt.xlabel('x axis')
plt.ylabel('y axis')
plt.title('Fitting data')
plt.legend()
plt.show()
            </code>
        </program>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
